NJ05 E3 independent retained-number size arithmetic, test-only
Source: exact-path seals in retained-number-sizes.sha256. R01 is the authored
ordered tree in retained-number.cases. E2-unknown is only the 14-key unknown
subtree of the sealed 683-byte old progress input, modeled as a generic root
map with kind progress and schema 2. It is not a full migrated progress
document; full E2 A/C literal bytes and adapter are NOT FROZEN.

Accounting order per line: total A/C bytes, header bytes, preorder-row bytes,
retained-number ASCII payload bytes. The final H row is always 74 bytes and
includes the digest and final LF. R payload is its token's ASCII byte count,
not the mathematical expansion of its value. Every row also counts tag, HT,
key, optional HT/payload and LF. A key/string counts canonical escaping;
C key/string counts U count plus four ASCII hex bytes per UTF-16 code unit.

R01 A = 316, 25, 217, 37
R01 C = 618, 26, 518, 37
E2-unknown A = 693, 25, 594, 37
E2-unknown C = 2051, 26, 1951, 37
R01 has 12 preorder rows, including an R list item. E2-unknown has 18 rows:
14 direct children and three nested R children. Its five direct numeric tokens
consume 34 bytes; nested z/a/m each contributes one, for 37 R payload bytes.

Previously frozen decoder-accepted old progress controls, inherited from
numeric-admission.cases/check_numeric_sizes.py, are physical output floors,
assuming the stated full progress projection; no E3 R value occurs in them:
PG-10000000 A = 10000112
PG-10000000 C = 40000022
PG-rows-32768 A = 10196726
PG-rows-32768 C = 39214145
PG-single durable-floor A = 20000112
PG-single durable-floor C = 50000022
PG-many durable-floor A = 20196726
PG-many durable-floor C = 49214145
The Desktop decoder accepted these 10,000,000-byte old inputs; required-host
persistence remains unproved. That input cap is not an output-byte cap. These
are lower floors only, not a chosen N26 ceiling or proof of host persistence.

Arithmetic negative controls: appending one ASCII digit to R01's wide numeric
token adds one byte to either arm's R row; replacing key wide by wïde adds one
UTF-8 byte to A, zero UTF-16 units to C; replacing E2's literal emoji by A
subtracts three A payload bytes and four C payload bytes. None changes a
frozen source or supplies a codec-derived expectation.

Resource accounting variables (bytes unless marked otherwise): L = sealed
old input; T = retained in-memory typed tree (host allocation, unmeasured);
O = canonical new text; Q = readback copy or stream buffer; B = any prior text
backup, if present; W = temporary staging copy; R = durable text receipt and
its staging, if present. Keep each term distinct. For R01 L is absent because
R01 is a synthetic tree; O is the calculated R01 arm total. For E2-unknown,
L = 683, O is only its generic subtree estimate, not a complete new document.
For PG-10000000, L = 10,000,000 and O is at least the arm's floor above.
Before verified cleanup, durable old plus complete new text alone requires
L + O bytes; E2-unknown's O is only a component of that complete new text;
other live B/W/R terms can increase disk peak according to the actual host
transaction. Materializing both old bytes and a full readback copy together
requires at least L + Q bytes of buffers. If full encoded output O and full
readback Q = O coexist with the old input and retained tree, the live terms
are L + T + 2O, before other host overhead. Streaming may avoid those copies,
so byte lengths do not establish an exact peak heap number. The transaction's
storage ordering, backup size, receipt syntax and host quota are unproved.
N14 domain, N16/N26 limit, N17 Kotlin duplicate-key preflight, P23 time and
logical steps, physical quota/peak memory on each host: NOT RUN.
